;;; animation-dom-reflow-not-motoi-subject.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; A broken-example is kept ON PURPOSE (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples teach the
;;; differences). This one is a DIALECT-LEAK, not a runtime failure: the three
;;; verbs below RUN, but they model a browser concept — DOM reflow policy and a
;;; per-frame layout time-budget — that has no meaning on Motoi's fantasy-console
;;; framebuffer. Chapter 04 (animation) documented these three DOM verbs as if
;;; "reflow policy" were the subject of animation. It is not. Real Motoi
;;; animation is the tween system (animation/tweens, animation/tick,
;;; animation/clear!) driven by easing curves (chapter 22) over interpolated
;;; positions (chapter 20) — the physics of a value moving through time, not the
;;; bookkeeping of a browser layout engine.
;;;
;;;   animation/budget            → a per-frame millisecond layout budget (DOM)
;;;   animation/reflow-policy     → 'auto / 'eager / 'deferred layout timing (DOM)
;;;   animation/set-reflow-policy → write that policy (DOM)
;;;
;;; These verbs are inherited from Curator's browser host, where a real DOM
;;; reflow costs real milliseconds and must be batched. Motoi paints into a
;;; framebuffer; there is no reflow, no layout pass, no millisecond budget to
;;; police. The subject of chapter 04 is now the real tween lifecycle.
;;;
;;; The three DOM verbs remain WIRED for the moment (lib/graphics/animation-budget.js);
;;; unwiring them is code surgery owned by #371 (rebuild motion/animation as real
;;; layers). This card records the model change on the DOCUMENTATION side (#372).

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — animation/* DOM-reflow verbs are not Motoi's animation subject")

(broken-example :name "animation/reflow-policy"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "dialect-leak-dom-reflow-vs-real-tween-and-easing"
  :std-target "Motoi animation/* (tween lifecycle) + easing/* + curve/*"
  :std-rejected ("animation/budget" "animation/reflow-policy" "animation/set-reflow-policy" "(Curator browser DOM-reflow surface)")
  :status "dialect-leak"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/animation-dom-reflow-not-motoi-subject.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (runs, but models the wrong world) ───
  :broken-code "(begin (animation/set-reflow-policy 'deferred) (animation/budget) (animation/reflow-policy))"
  :broken-output "auto — and note the tell: set-reflow-policy 'deferred is IGNORED; the read-back is a hard-coded 'auto, and budget is a hard-coded 16ms. These are inert inherited stubs, not a real policy engine."
  :runs "yes — but 'reflow policy' is a browser layout concept, not a fantasy-console animation concept, and here it is not even a working one — the write is a no-op"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"A DOM reflow is the browser recomputing element geometry after a style or
content change; it is expensive, so browsers let you batch it ('deferred') or
force it ('eager'), and animation code polices a per-frame millisecond budget so
reflows do not drop frames. NONE of that exists in Motoi. Motoi animation writes
pixels into a framebuffer and advances a tween clock — there is no layout tree to
reflow, no millisecond budget to spend, no policy to set. Documenting animation
through 'reflow policy' teaches the reader that animation is about layout
bookkeeping. It is not. Animation is a value moving through time: a start, an
end, a duration, and an easing curve that says HOW it moves between them."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(begin (animation/clear!) (motion/move-to (list 'star 0 0) (list 60 0)) (length (animation/tweens)))"
  :fix-output "1 — one live tween is now in flight toward (60 0)"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"The real animation surface is three verbs: animation/tweens (read the live
in-flight motions), animation/tick (advance one frame and redraw them), and
animation/clear! (drop them all). A tween is created by a motion verb
(motion/move-to). HOW the value moves between endpoints is an easing curve —
easing/linear, easing/standard, easing/accelerated, easing/decelerated,
easing/spring (chapter 22) — a monotone map [0,1]→[0,1]. WHERE it is at fraction
t is an interpolation, curve/eval (chapter 20). Its instantaneous velocity is
calc/derivative of the position curve (chapter 11). That is the real subject."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON ───
  :lesson
"When a verb's whole meaning comes from the HOST (a browser layout engine) and
not from Motoi's own machine (a framebuffer + a tween clock), it is a dialect
leak, not a Motoi verb. Port the SUBJECT, not the verb:
  · animation/budget answered 'how many milliseconds of layout may I spend this
    frame?' — a browser question. The Motoi question is 'how many tweens are
    live?' (length (animation/tweens)), a read of the real active set.
  · animation/reflow-policy / set-reflow-policy chose WHEN the browser recomputes
    geometry. Motoi has no geometry pass; the analogous real control is the
    easing curve, which chooses HOW a value moves, and animation/tick, which
    chooses WHEN the frame advances. Timing lives in the curve and the clock, not
    in a layout policy."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "Motoi animation/* + easing/*"
     :status "the wired target — tween lifecycle over easing curves"
     :code "(begin (animation/clear!) (motion/move-to (list 'dot 0 0) (list 100 0)) (animation/tick) (length (animation/tweens)))"
     :note "No policy, no budget: create a tween, tick the frame, read the live set. Easing (chapter 22) says how it moves; curve/eval (chapter 20) says where it is at fraction t.")
    (:lib "Pico-8"
     :status "no layout engine; animation is a value stepped each _update()"
     :code "function _update() x = x + (tx - x) * 0.2 end"
     :note "A fantasy console animates by moving a number toward a target every frame — exactly animation/tick advancing a tween. There is no reflow policy because there is no DOM.")
    (:lib "CSS transitions (the browser world these verbs came from)"
     :status "where reflow policy is REAL — kept here to show the boundary"
     :code "transition: transform 300ms cubic-bezier(.2,0,0,1);"
     :note "In a browser the easing lives in a cubic-bezier and reflow IS a real cost. Motoi keeps the easing curve (easing/standard ≈ this bezier) and drops the reflow policy, because a framebuffer has no layout pass to police.")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Target: chapter 04 documents animation/tweens / animation/tick / animation/clear!
(the real tween lifecycle, wired in lib/graphics/animation.js), taught through
easing/* (chapter 22), curve/eval (chapter 20), and calc/derivative (chapter 11).
The three DOM-reflow verbs (animation/budget, animation/reflow-policy,
animation/set-reflow-policy) dropped from the reference 2026-07-26; they remain
wired pending code surgery under #371.")
